<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<xmile version="1.0" level="3" xmlns="http://www.systemdynamics.org/XMILE" xmlns:isee="http://iseesystems.com/XMILE">
    <header>
        <smile version="1.0" namespace="std, isee">
            <uses_arrays>1</uses_arrays>
        </smile>
        <name>Lesson3MODELING</name>
        <uuid>a2beae7c-15b8-4216-b27e-7768faee3bb5</uuid>
        <vendor>isee systems, inc.</vendor>
        <product version="10.1.2" isee:saved_by_v1="false" lang="en">iThink</product>
    </header>
    <style>
        <model font-family="Arial" font-size="12" />
        <interface font-family="Arial" font-size="9" />
    </style>
    <sim_specs method="Euler" time_units="Days" isee:pause_after_rates="false" isee:instantaneous_flows="false">
        <start>0</start>
        <stop>5</stop>
        <dt>1</dt>
        <isee:analyze_mode>false</isee:analyze_mode>
    </sim_specs>
    <dimensions>
        <dim name="Dim_Name_1" size="1" />
    </dimensions>
    <model_units />
    <isee:prefs last_model="true" layer="model" grid_width="20" grid_height="50" divide_by_zero_alert="true" hide_taskbar="true" show_module_prefix="true">
        <isee:window width="858" height="611" />
        <isee:security />
        <isee:print_setup width="818" height="572" />
    </isee:prefs>
    <isee:equation_prefs order_by="module" />
    <model>
        <stock name="MY_ACCOUNT">
            <doc>In this simulation, the initial number of $$ in MY ACCOUNT equals my first day&apos;s costs for ingredients and supplies (or daily expenditures). First, you have to have the money to spend, then you earn money based on sales. </doc>
            <eqn>Expenses__Daily</eqn>
            <inflow>Daily_Income</inflow>
            <outflow>Daily_Expenses</outflow>
            <non_negative />
            <event_poster min="0" max="60">
                <threshold value="0.1" direction="decreasing">
                    <event sim_action="stop">
                        <text_box>You are out of money and cannot buy any more ingredients.  You must close your Lemonade Stand.  Better luck next time.</text_box>
                    </event>
                </threshold>
            </event_poster>
            <display x="360" y="1339" color="blue" />
        </stock>
        <flow name="Daily\nIncome">
            <doc>Your daily income is based on the number of cups you sell each day (daily cups actually sold) multiplied by the price you charge per cup. &#x0D;&#x0D;If you would like to change this equation, click &quot;Hide Document&quot; to view options. When you are satisfied with a new equation, click OK.</doc>
            <eqn>Daily_Cups_Actually_Sold * price_per_cup</eqn>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="283" y="1339" color="black" label_side="top">
                <pts>
                    <pt x="229" y="1339" />
                    <pt x="338" y="1339" />
                </pts>
            </display>
        </flow>
        <aux name="possible_number_of_\ncups_able_\nto_sell">
            <doc>If you click on &quot;To Graphical Function&quot; you will see the graph that describes how many cups can be sold for each price between $0.50 and $1.50 per cup. These are POSSIBLE sales, depending upon whether or not you have produced enough batches of lemonade to sell. &#x0D;&#x0D;You may change the graph. </doc>
            <eqn>price_per_cup</eqn>
            <gf>
                <xscale min="0.5" max="1.5" />
                <yscale min="0" max="300" />
                <ypts>300,225,150,75,55,40,32,24,16,8,0</ypts>
            </gf>
            <display x="166" y="1452" color="black" label_side="top" label_angle="103" />
        </aux>
        <stock name="TOTAL_PROFIT">
            <eqn>0</eqn>
            <outflow>Daily_Profit</outflow>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="375" y="1797" color="blue" />
        </stock>
        <stock name="MY\n_ACCOUNT">
            <doc>In this simulation, the initial number of $$ in MY ACCOUNT equals my first day&apos;s costs for ingredients and supplies (or daily expenditures). First, you have to have the money to spend, then you earn money based on sales. </doc>
            <eqn>daily__expenditures</eqn>
            <inflow>Daily__Income</inflow>
            <outflow>Daily__Expenses</outflow>
            <non_negative />
            <event_poster min="0" max="60">
                <threshold value="0.1" direction="decreasing">
                    <event sim_action="stop">
                        <text_box>You are out of money and cannot buy any more ingredients.  You must close your Lemonade Stand.  Better luck next time.</text_box>
                    </event>
                </threshold>
            </event_poster>
            <display x="363" y="829" color="red" />
        </stock>
        <flow name="Daily_\nIncome">
            <doc>The rate that Daily Income flows into MY ACCOUNT is determined by daily income from sales.&#x0D;&#x0D;If you would like to change this, click &quot;Hide Document&quot; to view options. When you are satisfied with a new equation, click OK.</doc>
            <eqn>daily__income_from_sales</eqn>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="286" y="829" color="black" label_side="top" label_angle="125">
                <pts>
                    <pt x="232" y="829" />
                    <pt x="341" y="829" />
                </pts>
            </display>
        </flow>
        <flow name="Daily\nExpenses">
            <doc>Total expenses each day are determined by the number of batches you choose to make multiplied by the cost per batch. &#x0D;&#x0D;If you would like to change this equation, click &quot;Hide Document&quot; to view options. When you are satisfied with a new equation, click OK.</doc>
            <eqn>batches_made * cost_per_batch</eqn>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="437" y="1338" color="red" label_side="top" label_angle="43">
                <pts>
                    <pt x="382" y="1338" />
                    <pt x="493" y="1338" />
                </pts>
            </display>
        </flow>
        <aux name="batches\nmade">
            <doc>In this simulation, you may choose to make between 1 and 4 batches (each of 75 cups of lemonade). &#x0D;&#x0D;Type in the number you want to change the number of batches made and click OK. </doc>
            <eqn>0</eqn>
            <display x="416" y="1440" color="fuchsia" label_side="left" label_angle="160" />
        </aux>
        <stock name="TOTAL_CUPS\nSOLD">
            <eqn>0</eqn>
            <inflow>Cups_Sold</inflow>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="101" y="1988" color="blue" />
        </stock>
        <flow name="Cups\nSold">
            <eqn>Daily_Cups_Actually_Sold</eqn>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="146" y="1987" color="blue" label_angle="305">
                <pts>
                    <pt x="170" y="1987" />
                    <pt x="123" y="1987" />
                </pts>
            </display>
        </flow>
        <aux name="price_per\ncup">
            <doc>This is the price (in $) you set for a cup of lemonade. &#x0D;&#x0D;In the simulation, this price could vary from 0.50 cents to $1.50 a cup. Type in a number to change the price per cup and click OK.</doc>
            <eqn>0</eqn>
            <event_poster min="1" max="2">
                <threshold value="1">
                    <event>
                        <text_box />
                    </event>
                </threshold>
            </event_poster>
            <display x="262" y="1426" color="fuchsia" />
        </aux>
        <stock name="TOTAL__EXPENSES">
            <eqn>0</eqn>
            <inflow>Expenses__Daily</inflow>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="637" y="1986" color="blue" label_angle="139" />
        </stock>
        <flow name="Expenses_\nDaily">
            <eqn>Daily_Expenses</eqn>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="693" y="1986" color="blue" label_angle="313">
                <pts>
                    <pt x="728" y="1986" />
                    <pt x="659" y="1986" />
                </pts>
            </display>
        </flow>
        <stock name="TOTAL__INCOME">
            <eqn>0</eqn>
            <inflow>Income_Daily</inflow>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="209" y="1795" color="blue" label_angle="126" />
        </stock>
        <aux name="Daily_Cups\nActually_Sold">
            <doc>This equation compares how many cups you COULD sell with the  number of cups you&apos;ve made (batches multiplied by 75 cups per batch) and picks the minimum number (you sell EITHER the amount you&apos;ve made or fewer cups if the price is too high).</doc>
            <eqn>ROUND(MIN(possible_number_of__cups_able__to_sell, (batches_made * 75)))</eqn>
            <display x="336" y="1472" color="black" label_angle="279" />
        </aux>
        <flow name="Daily_\nExpenses">
            <doc>The daily expenses that flow out of MY ACCOUNT are determined by my daily expenditures. &#x0D;&#x0D;If you would like to change this, click &quot;Hide Document&quot; to view options. When you are satisfied with a new equation, click OK.</doc>
            <eqn>daily__expenditures</eqn>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="440" y="828" color="red" label_side="top" label_angle="43">
                <pts>
                    <pt x="385" y="828" />
                    <pt x="496" y="828" />
                </pts>
            </display>
        </flow>
        <aux name="cost_per_batch">
            <doc>In the simulation, you may make between 1 and 4 batches of lemonade (each producing 75 cups of lemonade). &#x0D;&#x0D;The cost per batch falls as you make more batches. To see these costs in a graph, click on &quot;To Graphical Function.&quot;  You may change the numbers in this graph.</doc>
            <eqn>batches_made</eqn>
            <gf>
                <xscale min="1" max="4" />
                <yscale min="0" max="100" />
                <ypts>35,30,27,25</ypts>
            </gf>
            <display x="500" y="1429" color="red" />
        </aux>
        <flow name="Income\nDaily">
            <eqn>Daily_Income</eqn>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="265" y="1799" color="blue" label_side="top" label_angle="63">
                <pts>
                    <pt x="300" y="1799" />
                    <pt x="231" y="1799" />
                </pts>
            </display>
        </flow>
        <stock name="TOTAL_CUPS\nDISCARDED">
            <eqn>0</eqn>
            <inflow>Cups_Discarded</inflow>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="336" y="1989" color="blue" />
        </stock>
        <flow name="Cups\nDiscarded">
            <eqn>(batches_made * 75) - Daily_Cups_Actually_Sold</eqn>
            <non_negative />
            <display x="395" y="1991" color="blue" label_side="top" label_angle="45">
                <pts>
                    <pt x="433" y="1991" />
                    <pt x="358" y="1991" />
                </pts>
            </display>
        </flow>
        <aux name="daily_\nexpenditures">
            <doc>How much are my daily expenditures?&#x0D;&#x0D;In the simulation, this depends on how many batches of lemonade you make. That information is contained in a graph found in a  &quot;cost per batch&quot; converter in the &quot;Model Used in Lesson 3.&quot;&#x0D;&#x0D;Type in a number to change daily expenditures and click OK. </doc>
            <eqn>0</eqn>
            <display x="474" y="880" color="red" label_angle="295" />
        </aux>
        <aux name="TOTAL___CUPS_SOLD">
            <eqn>TOTAL_CUPS_SOLD+Cups_Sold</eqn>
            <display x="104" y="2042" color="blue" />
        </aux>
        <aux name="TOTAL___CUPS_DISCARDED">
            <eqn>TOTAL_CUPS_DISCARDED+Cups_Discarded</eqn>
            <display x="340" y="2067" color="blue" />
        </aux>
        <aux name="TOTAL_INCOME">
            <eqn>TOTAL__INCOME+Income_Daily</eqn>
            <display x="208" y="1875" color="blue" />
        </aux>
        <aux name="TOTAL_EXPENSES">
            <eqn>TOTAL__EXPENSES+Expenses__Daily</eqn>
            <display x="598" y="2038" color="blue" />
        </aux>
        <aux name="daily__income\nfrom_sales">
            <doc>How much money do you make from daily sales?&#x0D;&#x0D; In the simulation, your daily income from sales is based on the price you set for a cup of lemonade. This ranges from 300 cups being sold at a price of 50 cents, to none at $1.50 per cup. This information can be found in a graph inside the &quot;possible number of cups able to sell&quot; converter in the &quot;Model Used in Lesson 3.&quot;&#x0D;&#x0D;Type in a new number to change daily income from sales and click OK.</doc>
            <eqn>0</eqn>
            <display x="251" y="885" color="black" label_angle="253" />
        </aux>
        <flow name="Daily_Profit">
            <doc>Your daily profit is calculated by taking the amount of money you make in sales minus the expenses incurred in buying supplies. </doc>
            <eqn>Daily_Income - Daily_Expenses</eqn>
            <display x="430" y="1805" color="blue" label_side="top" label_angle="63">
                <pts>
                    <pt x="397" y="1805" />
                    <pt x="463" y="1805" />
                </pts>
            </display>
        </flow>
        <aux name="hours_worked\nper_day">
            <doc>In this simulation, each batch of lemonade you make requires 2 hours to produce and sell. Hence the number of batches made multiplied by 2 hours produces the total number of hours you work each day. &#x0D;&#x0D;Click &quot;Hide Document&quot; if you would like to change this equation. </doc>
            <eqn>batches_made * 2</eqn>
            <display x="440" y="1513" color="blue" />
        </aux>
        <aux name="profit\nper_hour">
            <doc>An interesting statistic is how much profit you make for each hour you work. You calculate that by taking total profit and dividing it by numbers of hours worked. </doc>
            <eqn>if hours_worked_per_day = 0 then 0 else Daily_Profit / hours_worked_per_day</eqn>
            <display x="513" y="1526" color="blue" label_side="right" label_angle="348" />
        </aux>
        <display page_width="818" page_height="572" page_cols="2" page_rows="4" isee:converter_size="medium" isee:name_only_modules="true" isee:animate_flows="true" isee:animate_stocks="true" interval="DT" report_flows="summed">
            <connector x="173" y="1457" uid="0" color="black">
                <from>possible_number_of__cups_able__to_sell</from>
                <to>Daily_Cups_Actually_Sold</to>
            </connector>
